WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.832
In July 1935 an American vice consul in Shanghai pinned a small photograph to a

2
00:00:05.832 --> 00:00:11.364
form, and an eighteen-year-old student signed his name across it: Ieoh-ming Pe.

3
00:00:11.714 --> 00:00:15.174
I. M. Pei came to America on a two-year student visa.

4
00:00:15.524 --> 00:00:18.368
For twelve years the government expected him to go home.

5
00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.940
He stayed, became one of the most celebrated architects in

6
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.162
the world, and put a glass pyramid in front of the Louvre.

7
00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:31.062
His immigration file, all 139 pages of it, survives at the National Archives.

8
00:00:31.412 --> 00:00:34.453
To change from a visiting student to a permanent resident, Pei

9
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:37.739
and his wife had to leave the United States and apply from outside.

10
00:00:38.089 --> 00:00:42.118
Twelve years after he arrived, he was finally allowed to stay.

11
00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:45.184
Read the full story, with every source, on this page.
